[00:00] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A hand holds a small antenna, likely for a radio.
AUDIO: 'Your phone is great at keeping you connected as long as you keep paying the bill. But soon you might be able to stay connected across continents and do it all over a network you actually own. Something the legacy telecom model still can\'t give you. This radio doesn\'t need'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a cell tower and you don\'t need to pay a monthly subscription for the right to use it. You can modify it, upcycle the hardware, and extend what it can do. It transmits on a frequency about 100 times lower than traditional Wi-Fi. The same'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of radio people use to carry their voices across oceans. But instead of talking into an analog microphone, I\'m bridging it into Reticulum, a fully digital, permissionless, open-source network. I want to find out if we can'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connect communities hundreds or even thousands of miles apart over the air for free and help build an internet that belongs to the people using it. The problem is today our communication passes through gatekeepers. They decide'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what we\'re allowed to do, keep a record of us doing it, and then charge us for the privilege. I want to build a network where nobody has that kind of power over the people using it. Which might sound like wishful thinking, but incredibly'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cheap, low power technology, open- source code, and communityrun networks are already functioning today. Projects like Meshtastic and Meshcore have proven out that model and continue to gain'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adoption. In fact, a few weeks ago, I was driving north to see some family from Central Florida all the way to New Hampshire. And for fun, I stuck a magnetic 915 megahertz whip antenna on the back of my car with a small Neo1'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'listening on the public Longfast channel. And over the course of the trip, the radio picked up messages and connected us with people along the route. Some cities like conquered New Hampshire were absolutely teeming with'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'activity. And by the end of the trip, we had seen more than 450 nodes on just that one protocol. That\'s what this looks like when enough people get curious, put up a radio, and invite somebody else to try it. You start with'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something you want to understand, share it with a few friends, and little by little, a community builds around it. But what happens when the people you want to reach are hundreds of miles away? That\'s the gap I\'ve been trying to bridge for over a year now. Because'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right now, if you want long range communication, you basically have three options. You can use cell towers, but you need a large number of them, and they\'re really only economically viable for big telos and ISPs. You can use a'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'satellite service like Starlink, Garmin, or Aridium. These are good for off-grid situations. And in my last video, I showed how you could hook an Aridium modem into Reticulum and send a text'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'message from anywhere with a clear view of the sky. But the satellites were proprietary. You need to broker a monthly plan, and each message still incurred a toll. Or you can build a community network with something like'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Meshtastic. And that model is real, local, and actually belongs to the people using it. But covering serious distance requires a lot of nodes and enough people between here and wherever you\'re trying to reach. But is there'

[02:54] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A Baofeng handheld radio is shown with its display active.
AUDIO: 'another option? I think so. And if this works, privatelyowned networks could connect across hundreds or even thousands of miles without traditional internet back haul. But for that to have even the slightest chance of being'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'possible, I need two miracles. Miracle number one is I need to somehow cross this on a path we own. And miracle number two, I need to do it legally. Now, the physics behind this isn\'t new,'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and neither is sending data over radio. What is new is reticulum and who can build with it. Radios that once cost thousands are suddenly becoming affordable. Open source networking software can stitch completely different'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'links into one single network. And AI lets one reasonably stubborn person work through problems that once required an entire engineering team. For the first time, something like this doesn\'t feel completely insane. But that doesn\'t mean'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s a great idea. After all, maybe there\'s a reason nobody has done this before. But really, there\'s only one way to find out. And fortunately for you, circumventing corporate and government overreach is one of my favorite hobbies. So, I\'m going to try it. one real'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reticulum message, hundreds, maybe thousands of miles. No cell tower, private satellite or corporate network in the middle. And the technology that makes that possible is one a lot of people have already written off. This is'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ham radio. And depending on who you ask, ham radio is either one of the last longrange communication systems ordinary people can actually own or an elaborate way for retired men to discuss the weather. But these radios can do'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something your Wi-Fi router can\'t. They can potentially cross this, but they can carry digital data, too. Small packets sent directly over the air. Could one of those packets travel thousands of miles? Potentially. But could we turn that'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connection into a bridge for a modern open-source network? That\'s what I\'m here to find out. But I wasn\'t here to chat with random operators. I wanted to take the thing making those conversations possible, the radio link itself, and make software talk through'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it instead. Because ham radio isn\'t limited to voice. That noise is data. It\'s called APRS. Small packets carrying locations, weather reports, status updates, and'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'short messages. Nearby stations can repeat those packets while internet gateways collect them and plot their reported positions on maps like this. So, sending data over amateur radio isn\'t new. But APRS was built for a'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different job. And this particular APRS channel is on VHF. Great for local coverage, but not the long-distance Skywave link that I\'m attempting. I could build another oneoff HF messaging'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system, but then it would connect only radio operators running the same hardware and software. That\'s why I want to use Reticulum. Reticulum is transport agnostic. It doesn\'t care whether data travels over Laura, Wi-Fi, Ethernet, the'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'internet, or a custom HF link to Reticulum. Each one is simply another path through the network. So, this wouldn\'t create a tiny new network for a handful of HF operators. It could bridge'

[05:51] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A slide displays a "TRADITIONAL HF RADIO" with a product photo of a Yaesu FTDX10 transceiver.
AUDIO: 'existing reticulum communities together, even if the people inside those communities never touch an HF radio themselves. Someone using Laura in one town could potentially reach someone on'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an entirely different kind of link hundreds of miles away with HF quietly carrying the data across the gap. That means I need to design a custom HF packet that carries what our bridge needs. If it works, this radio doesn\'t'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'become another isolated system. It becomes a long-distance bridge between networks that already exist. Now, I have a bad habit of refusing to ship these projects until they\'re absolutely'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perfect. Which is why you sometimes wait months between videos. So, this time I gave myself 3 weeks. When Sunday comes, testing stops. Whether it works or not, I have to ship what I have. So, I have 3 weeks to build the bridge. To attempt'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this, I need one more radio, which is saying something because my office is already full of them. I have boards for Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, Laura, Halo, even an Aridium modem. But those radios either stay relatively local or depend on'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'somebody else\'s infrastructure to go farther. This is the Airmemes Light 2, and it\'s the key to this entire puzzle. It\'s a relatively inexpensive open-source softwaredefined radio rated'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to cover frequencies from 0 to 38.4 4 MHz including the part of the spectrum known as HF or high frequency. A traditional HF radio is built primarily around a person with a microphone, but'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Airme is built around a computer. It can still carry voice, but I can also have my software generate the exact data frame I need, put it directly on the air, and decode it on the other side.'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And HF has a trick that none of the other radios scattered around my office can perform on their own. Under the right conditions, an HF signal can travel into the ionosphere, reflect back'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'toward the Earth, and land hundreds or even thousands of miles beyond the horizon. It\'s called skywave propagation. There is no chain of community nodes or private satellite carrying the messages. The atmosphere'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'itself becomes part of the path. So, this might be the right radio that lets us cross this map. There was just one minor problem. Mine arrived in three pieces. Okay, so let\'s try to put this guy together. So, we have the enclosure,'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we have the main uh board, and then we have the filter board, which we\'re also going to need. So, three parts, and that should be all we need to put this guy together. So, let\'s go ahead and open'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these up. You slide the board into these uh into these rails here. I kind of like that. Um, so basically this goes on like this. There\'s nothing holding it'

[08:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
