[09:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just you can go right on top. Um, and I think we\'re going to be good to go. Um, no, I think we\'re good though. This thing is uh is pretty solid. We\'ve got it set up here. Good to go. Was perhaps'

[09:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slightly optimistic because while the was now capable of transmitting, I still wasn\'t legally allowed to press that button. Remember, miracle number two, I have to do this all legally.'

[09:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Transmitting data on this part of the amateur radio spectrum requires a license, and I didn\'t have one yet. But receiving is different. Anyone can listen. So, while I studied for the ham radio technician class license exam, I'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'started learning about the receive side of the radio. And a lot of that studying came from two YouTube channels, Ham Radio Crash Course and Ham Radio 2.0. I basically had their videos playing in the background while I tinkered with the'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rig. So, thank you to both of them. The first thing I learned was that the radio itself was only half the problem. To hear anything, I needed the right antenna. The 900 MHz radios I normally use have a wavelength of about 13 in,'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but at 28 MHz, the wavelength is roughly 35 ft. The antenna doesn\'t have to be a full wavelength long, but it does scale with it, which is why this thing has a much longer antenna. This is a Falcon'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dipole. basically two lengths of wire sized for the band. Ideally, it would be outside high in the air with plenty of open space. You\'ll notice my installation took a slightly more'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interpretive approach. Okay, so this thing is a nano VNA and it helps you understand how effective your Intuna setup is. This thing intimidated me for a long time. You can see there\'s a lot going on here and it\'s not clear what'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything does. So, I\'m going to simplify it for you. So what we can do is we can remove some of these traces. Display trace. We can get rid of this. We can get rid of this and we can get'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rid of this. What we really care about is the SWR which tells us where the center frequency is for the antenna and how well tuned it is at that frequency. So and we do need to specify a range.'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But basically what we can do is we can take an antenna like this. So, this is a Muzzy Works 915 megahertz whip antenna. So, what we can do is we can just screw this on like so. And it\'s going to start'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to give us a readout here. We have the end of the range here at 900. So, what we\'re going to want to do is move this so that we can actually see um what we want. So we\'ll do start at say 850 MHz and then end stop at 960'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MGHertz. Let\'s see here. Okay, we can start to see the center frequency. And so now if we want to see like exactly where that dip is, we just drag this'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over, bring it down to the dip. So you can see you can see it moves a little bit like based on like how you know how we have this thing set up. But we can see it\'s around 900 MHz, right? Um, one other thing I\'ll show you is, um, you'

[12:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probably have some triband antennas or dualband antennas. Um, this right here is one of those. Just had to get a special connector for this, but um, when you put in an antenna that is dual band'

[12:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or triband or what have you, you\'ll get multiple sort of like resonance zones. If I\'m transmitting on 28 MHz, is my antenna actually tuned around 28 MHz and does it have an efficient reading? Yeah,'

[12:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these things aren\'t that intimidating. You can get better results by calibrating it each time with these three sort of dummy loads right here. You pop it on, go over to uh calibrate, and then you hit calibrate. You have'

[13:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open, short, load, and you just do all three of those and it\'ll calibrate it. So, pretty easy. Definitely a useful tool in my toolkit to see like how effective an actual antenna is. and it\'s been interesting. &gt;&gt; Um, now one of the cool things is you'

[13:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can get that to move based on like moving the arms of the antenna. So like see how the center right now says 26.840. Well, if we see one is clipped over'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there and one is clipped over there. If I move like let\'s uncip it real quick. So and I just tossed it down there. And now you can see it\'s much deeper just by'

[13:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moving it. Right. But the problem is we want the center frequency to move to 28. That\'s the problem. Yeah. You can see how like moving the arms affects'

[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the shape there. So like now it\'s more towards 28.160 which is where we\'re going to transmit. If you zoom out a little bit like you can see it\'s relatively flat flat flat flat but then when we get to our 28 MHz'

[14:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'re we\'re dipping down and that\'s basically what we want. So that\'s really just the process that I used to get these antennas tuned properly, but it was connected and somehow it started hearing things. So this is my first day'

[14:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the Airme and I have Spark SDR going here and I didn\'t get any voice uh well I did get a little bit of voice but not a whole lot um when I started'

[14:37] [scene:environment]
VISUAL: A cluttered floor with various electronic components and boxes.
AUDIO: 'playing around with it. But I\'m listening right now on this band here, um, 21 MHz. Um, the channel that I\'m ultimately going to use or the frequency I\'m ultimately going to use is 28 MHz, uh, which is what my antenna is tuned'

[14:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for. But nonetheless, um, when I listen on this frequency uh, and decoding FT8 data packets, I see basically a whole conversation going on here. FT8 is a'

[15:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'digital mode built for what\'s called weak signal communication. It makes the same basic trade-off as Laura, a very low data rate in exchange for working at extremely low signal to noise ratios,'

[15:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sometimes recovering messages from signals you can barely distinguish from the noise. It\'s basically lightweight text messaging over HF. And um the first message that I got was actually from'

[15:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Bogotaa, Colombia over HF. And this right here is what a message looks like. It\'s like a thin line goes out for several seconds and then it gets decoded and then it gets turned into the actual'

[15:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'message here. And this is like the call sign the station and it\'s basically saying like received and then it\'s giving a a dB like received at -14 dB and uh you have the time stamp and all'

[15:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that stuff here. So like my first message uh I received from Bogota, Colombia. I\'m here in uh central Florida. That\'s 1600 miles. The air may right here. The antenna setup I have is'

[16:09] [scene:talk]
